tc.research_name.TEMPERED_THAUMIUM=Tempered Thaumium
tc.research_text.TEMPERED_THAUMIUM=A metal that holds both current and magic
unboundtech.research_page.TEMPERED_THAUMIUM.1=Tempering is always a loss.<BR><BR>The blast furnace holds thaumium at a heat no crucible ever reached, under a pressure that rebuilds its lattice. Strength arrives, but part of the Praecantatio burns away: what was an even lilac glow sets into rare violet flecks in a grey body.<BR><BR>Alchemists call it spoilage, engineers call it stabilisation. Both are right: no wand will ever come of this metal, but it holds a machine casing without buckling under current.
unboundtech.research_page.TEMPERED_THAUMIUM.2=There are two roads, and they come from opposite sides.<BR><BR>The magical road: take a finished thaumium ingot and send it into the blast furnace. Five minutes and a tankful of compressed air - exactly what the furnace asks for ordinary steel, not a tick longer.<BR><BR>The technical road: take a steel ingot and drop it into the crucible. Steel is already dead; magic is not poured into it but fused in - hence the stabilisers. Permutatio rebuilds the lattice, Vitreus holds the shape as it cools. The recipe is on the next page.<BR><BR>The ingot is the same either way, and both stack together. Take whichever road runs through the half of your workshop that already exists.

tc.research_name.TEMPERED_ARMOR=Forged Plate
tc.research_text.TEMPERED_ARMOR=Armour you hammer, not armour you infuse
unboundtech.research_page.TEMPERED_ARMOR.1=Fortress armour is forged on an altar, and the vis lays each plate where it belongs. Tempered thaumium allows no such thing: its shape was set by the furnace, not by will. The smith swings a hammer where a thaumaturge would merely wish - and you can see it the moment you pick the suit up. It is crude. The plates are thicker, the seams show, the runes ran crooked and hold vis worse than they might have.<BR><BR>On the other hand, you can wear it the day after your furnace starts.<BR><BR>Count it honestly. Twenty-four armour across the set against diamond's twenty, and that is the only line you win. Toughness is halved, the enchanting table offers short dull lists, and every casting in this suit costs a fifth more. This is not a better diamond suit. It is a different one.

tc.research_name.TEMPERED_TOOLS=Tempered Thaumium Tools
tc.research_text.TEMPERED_TOOLS=A workhorse, not a wand
unboundtech.research_page.TEMPERED_TOOLS.1="Tempered thaumium remembers both the anvil and the crucible. A tool made of it does not cut stone - it persuades it: no sparks, no shriek, just the stubbornness of metal that has already survived a blast furnace. Mages complain there is little magic left in it. It will outlive three of their wands."
unboundtech.research_page.TEMPERED_TOOLS.2=Where it stands in the line, by the numbers.<BR><BR>Thaumium tools: 500 durability, enchantability 18, the occasional favour from the metal.<BR>Void: 600 durability, enchantability 20, mends itself.<BR>Diamond: 1561 durability, enchantability 10.<BR>Ours: 1873 durability, enchantability 10, no favours at all.<BR><BR>So it outlasts everything a thaumaturge owns, and beats diamond by a fifth. It was paid for with research, a long haul of metalwork and a ceiling on enchanting: no amount of books will wring a good pickaxe out of it. There are no special effects and there will not be - that ground belongs to the order's own tools.

tc.research_name.ELECTRIC_SCRIBING=Electric Scribing Tools
tc.research_text.ELECTRIC_SCRIBING=A pen that writes with a discharge
unboundtech.research_page.ELECTRIC_SCRIBING.1=A pen that writes not with ink but with a discharge: the needle leaves a thin scorched line on the parchment, and such notes outlast handwritten ones. A charge does not last long — but it returns for nothing, so long as an engineers' charging pad stands nearby. They find this obvious; my colleagues find it humiliating.<BR><BR>Four thousand of their units of power, fifty for every insight, eighty notes from a full charge. An ordinary inkwell wins by its reserves; this one wins because its reserves need no husbanding.


# --- Machine screens ---

# --- "How to use" pages ---
unboundtech.research_page.TEMPERED_THAUMIUM.3=I have walked both roads myself.<BR><BR>The blast furnace takes a thaumium ingot as readily as iron: five minutes of heat, a tankful of compressed air, and out of the fire comes a grey metal flecked with frozen lilac. The crucible asks differently: a steel ingot, four measures of Praecantatio and a pair each of Permutatio and Vitreus — steel is already dead, and magic must be fused into it, not poured.<BR><BR>From this metal I forge plate and tool alike; the casings of both resonance engines are built of it too. The anvil takes it like an old friend, and mends it with the same ingot.<BR><BR>The wrench, however, is forged of living thaumium: its jaws need memory of shape, not stubbornness. A light touch turns a machine about; a firm press lifts it whole, with everything it has gathered. The engineers' machinery obeys it as readily as my own.

unboundtech.research_page.TEMPERED_ARMOR.2=Forged at a plain bench in the familiar layout: five ingots for the helm, eight for the breastplate, seven for the greaves, four for the boots — twenty-four for the full suit. The anvil mends it with the same metal, though rough reforging shakes the enchantments loose.<BR><BR>I felt the price the very first evening: in the full suit every casting draws a fifth more from the wand than it ought. To one who does not cast, the armour costs nothing — save for how grudgingly the burnt-out metal accepts a charm.

unboundtech.research_page.TEMPERED_TOOLS.3=Forged like any tool: three ingots and two hafts for the pick and for the axe, two and one for the blade, one and two for the spade, two and two for the hoe.<BR><BR>The pick bites obsidian — whatever yields to diamond yields to it. The anvil mends it with a tempered ingot. Charms settle on it grudgingly: the enchanting table offers short, miserly lists, and the best I ever managed came through infusion.<BR><BR>This metal grants no favours. All its virtue is stubbornness.

unboundtech.research_page.ELECTRIC_SCRIBING.2=I keep it among the rest of my writing things. The research table reaches for the first inkwell it finds — the old one, to avoid confusion, went into a chest.<BR><BR>Any charger of the engineers restores it, from the humble battery box to their tall vaults. The glow of the vial tells how much writing remains; when it runs dry it neither spoils nor empties for good — it lies mute until returned to the current.



# --- Tier T3: Flux Condenser ---
tile.unboundtech.flux_condenser.name=Flux Condenser
item.unboundtech.flux_charge.name=Flux Charge
unboundtech.tooltip.flux_charge=Safe to carry. Unwise to drop

tc.research_name.FLUX_CONDENSER=Flux Condenser
tc.research_text.FLUX_CONDENSER=Corruption pays its debts
unboundtech.research_page.FLUX_CONDENSER.1=The murk the scrubber boils off is not refuse — it is frightened magic. It strains toward anywhere at all, and if one offers it copper coils instead of empty air, it goes into them almost gratefully. The Mechanists joke that this is the only case on record of corruption paying its debts.<BR><BR>Scrubbing flux used to be a chore: the scrubber demanded vis and attention, and hoarded murk nobody wanted. Now the chain has an end — and the end has a meter.
unboundtech.research_page.FLUX_CONDENSER.2=The device stands behind the scrubber: a pipe from its face, and the two of them settle the rest between themselves. The condenser draws only the frightened Praecantatio; any other aspect in the pipe simply does not move.<BR><BR>A unit of murk becomes two thousand units of the engineers' power, down a low-voltage line like a modest generator. No fortune will come of it: the scrubber yields murk grudgingly, and the flow runs thinner than any honest machine's. It is payment for the chore, not a vein of ore.<BR><BR>When the condenser has nowhere to put its own current it stops drawing — and the murk waits patiently in the pipe, losing nothing.
unboundtech.research_page.FLUX_CONDENSER.3=A light touch of the wrench turns it to its other work — thickening. Four measures of murk and a little of its own current, and the device pushes out a heavy faceted clot: a Flux Charge.<BR><BR>It is the only known way to carry corruption in a pocket. The clot is calm while whole; but fire and lava split the casing, and a cloud of flux gas rises where it perished. The arquebus masters have already asked after these charges — there is ammunition in them.<BR><BR>Do not mistake thickening for profit: every clot forgoes eight thousand units of power and spends two more atop. Corruption grows dearer when tamed.

unboundtech.research_page.THAUMODYNAMIC_TABLES.4=Added later, in a different ink.<BR><BR>Murk from the scrubber: 2,000 EU a unit — the condenser, the end of the flux chain.<BR>A Flux Charge: four murk and two thousand of current, ten thousand in equivalence.<BR><BR>Murk is the only aspect the machines will take back. All else, once turned essentia, stays essentia.

# --- Tier T3: Thaumic Overclocker ---
item.unboundtech.thaumic_overclocker.name=Thaumic Overclocker
unboundtech.tooltip.overclocker.time=time
unboundtech.tooltip.overclocker.power=power draw
unboundtech.tooltip.overclocker.hot=Overheated, ticks to cool
unboundtech.tooltip.overclocker.empty=Essentia spent — machine at its own pace

tc.research_name.THAUMIC_OVERCLOCKER=Thaumic Overclocker
tc.research_text.THAUMIC_OVERCLOCKER=Speed for essentia, not for shouting
unboundtech.research_page.THAUMIC_OVERCLOCKER.1=Copper and redstone make a mechanism hurry by shouting current at it. Thaumium goes another way: it shows the mechanism what it could be — and the mechanism hurries of its own accord. This costs less than shouting; but the sight makes the machine run hot, and when it runs hot too long, that familiar murk seeps from the seams.<BR><BR>The price is taken out of the current entirely: the device drinks Machina and Motus — a measure of each for every half-minute of zeal. When the essentia runs dry the mechanism simply returns to its own pace, not a whit slower than before.
unboundtech.research_page.THAUMIC_OVERCLOCKER.2=A comparison, written down after a week at the macerator.<BR><BR>The engineers' plate drives a machine to seven tenths of its time and demands six tenths more current on top. Mine drives it to six tenths and asks a fifth. All told, an operation costs seventy-two hundredths of its old price — faster AND cheaper, at any count of plates.<BR><BR>They may be mixed with the engineers' kind, the multipliers honestly multiply — but it is a poor bargain: their plate still drinks current the old way. Choose a school.<BR><BR>A machine takes four. Four means fourfold thirst, and fourfold murk when they overheat.
unboundtech.research_page.THAUMIC_OVERCLOCKER.3=On overheating, learned the hard way.<BR><BR>An hour of unbroken zeal and the plate goes mute: for half a minute the machine returns to its own pace, and a cloud of flux gas rises above it. Then everything resumes on its own.<BR><BR>A workshop labouring at half strength never overheats: rest cools twice as fast as work warms. Unbroken zeal loses less than a tenth of its winnings to the muting — but the murk comes and comes, and it must go somewhere. Thus one device drags the next behind it.<BR><BR>Refill by touching a jar of Machina or Motus; the device takes no other aspect. Pulling a hot plate to cool it in a pocket will not do: the heat is locked inside and fades only above a resting machine.

unboundtech.research_page.FLUX_CONDENSER.4=A note in the margin: murk comes from more than the scrubber. An overheated overclocker plate breathes it out right above its machine — and a condenser nearby turns that lapse into current too. Greed, chores and profit have closed into a ring.

# --- Tier T3: Resonant Splitter ---
tile.unboundtech.resonant_splitter.name=Resonant Splitter

tc.research_name.RESONANT_SPLITTER=Resonant Splitter
tc.research_text.RESONANT_SPLITTER=Both quarrellers walk away whole
unboundtech.research_page.RESONANT_SPLITTER.1=A compound aspect is not a blend but a quarrel: two meanings stuck together out of weariness. The centrifuge parts them by brawling, and the loser perishes. Resonance is politer — it gives each quarreller a note of its own, and both walk away whole.<BR><BR>The notes, admittedly, must be bought with electricity.
unboundtech.research_page.RESONANT_SPLITTER.2=The ledger against the old centrifuge, drawn up over an evening at both.<BR><BR>The centrifuge takes a unit of compound and returns one component — whichever wins; the other dies in the scuffle. Free, but half goes nowhere.<BR><BR>The splitter takes the same unit and returns BOTH components, always and predictably — at six thousand units of the engineers' power per parting. The pace is identical: thirty-nine heartbeats to the unit.<BR><BR>From ten units of Metallum the old machine makes ten random halves; resonance makes ten Terra and ten Vitreus, exactly.
unboundtech.research_page.RESONANT_SPLITTER.3=Notes on handling.<BR><BR>It will not take the primal essences at all: there is nothing in them to part, and no suction is declared for them. A pipe of Ignis passes by without noticing it.<BR><BR>The output holds eight measures of each component; when there is nowhere to put even one of them, the work does not begin at all. This is deliberate: to begin and discard the second component would be to become that very centrifuge.<BR><BR>An empty phial pressed to the casing carries off eight measures of whichever component has gathered more. A vein of redstone mutes the resonance — same as with the old machine.

